   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to designating and nominating petitions",
    "basePrintNo": "S1421",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1421-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§6-132, 6-140, 6-204, 6-206 & 15-108, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1421\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to designating and\nnominating petitions\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis legislation clarifies the requirements for designating and\nnominating petitions.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Subdivision 1 of section 6-132 of the Election Law is\namended by adding a space to include a district number, if applicable,\non each sheet of a Designating Petition.\n\nSection 2. Paragraph a of subdivision 1 of section 6-140 of the\nElection Law is amended by adding the directive to \"include a district\nnumber, if applicable\" on each sheet of an Independent Nominating\nPetition.\n\nSection 3. Subdivision 1 of section 6-204 of the Election Law is\namended by adding the directive \"include a district number, if\napplicable\" on a Party Designating Petition for elective village\noffices.\n\nSection 4. Subdivision 1 of section 6-206 of the Election Law is\namended by adding the directive \"include district number, if\napplicable\" on the petition for a Village Independent Nominating\nPetition.\n\nSection 5. Paragraph a of subdivision 3 and subdivision 4 of section\n15-108 of the Election Law is amended by adding the directive to\n\"include district number, if applicable\".\n\nSection 6. Establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe decision to vote is intrinsically linked to the choice of\ncandidates on the ballot. The right to become a candidate in an\nelection is as important as the right to vote. An individual can\npetition to become a candidate. However, many petitions have been\ninvalidated because the public office -the title of the office and\ngeographic boundaries (the district number)-was not clearly identified\nin the petition. For example, in the matter of Osaretin Ighile versus\nBoard of Elections in the City of New York, the Supreme Court declined\nto validate the petition because the description of public office was\ndeemed inadequate; the district number was not on the petition. This\nlegislation intends to clarify the information that is required on\neach sheet of the petition. By creating a designation for the\ngeographic boundaries on the sample petitions provided by the New York\nState Board of Elections, individuals will be aware of what the\nrequired information is, and thus be better able to participate in the\ndemocratic process as candidates.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is new legislation.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThere are no fiscal implications associated with this legislation.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
